0349: SweetFramebuffer: display engine registers and CTL_FLUSH latch, read only (kern.sweet_fb_regs, kern.sweet_fb_latch)

Diagnostics only: no register is written that was not written before. The bootloader (continuous splash)
sets up the display engine; the kernel only points VIG0 at its own buffers (scanFrom(): SRC0_ADDR,
SRC_YSTRIDE0, CTL_FLUSH = 0x20001). 0350 needs to know the plane the bootloader left and how the MDP takes a
flush.

kern.sweet_fb_regs (read only, ~12 KB of text): MDP TOP (0x5e01000, 0x494 bytes), CTL0 (0x5e02000, 0x1dc),
VIG0 (0x5e05000, 0x1f8) with its QSEED3LITE scaler (0x5e05a00, 0xa0) and CSC (0x5e06a00, 0x100), LM0
(0x5e45000, 0x320), PP0 (0x5e71000, 0xd4) and INTF1 (0x5e6b800, 0x2c0), 8 words a line, plus VIG0 and its
scaler as they were in start(). Offsets and sizes from the phone's device tree and Linux's
catalog/dpu_6_3_sm6115.h; Linux's dpu_kms_mdp_snapshot reads the same blocks.

kern.sweet_fb_latch (read only): each flip is followed until CTL_FLUSH reads its bits clear (by the frame
queue's thread and the ticks): what reads back right after the write, time to clear, how far
INTF_FRAME_COUNT had moved by then (per sixteenth of the refresh at the write), and flips not seen clear in
100 ms. Linux and the stock driver take a flip as done when those bits read clear after the vblank; 0340
takes it as done when the frame counter has moved.

SweetSurface logs the property names and creator of the first 16 screen-sized surfaces ("SWEET SURF:
screen-sized surface N by P: keys").

Measured on the phone (one boot, 14388 flips): the bits read set right after the write in 14386 flips and
clear at the programmable fetch (line 2420 of 2432), ~12 lines before INTF_FRAME_COUNT moves.

